ct (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0021951726003210)\nciteturn4search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; Available online 22 May 2026, 116986 ... “One-Stone-Two-Birds” catalysis overcomes single-substrate activation: parallel dual-activation on a Cu_{3} nanocluter for efficient CO_{2} utilization\n\n## Journal of Catalysis\n\nAvailable online 22 May 2026, 116986\n\nIn Press, Journal Pre-proofWhat’s this?\n\n# \n\nResearch article\n\n“One-Stone-Two-Birds” catalysis overcomes single-substrate activation: parallel dual-activation on a Cu_{3} nanocluter for efficient CO_{2} utilization\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.jcat.2026.116986Get rights and content\n\n## Highlights:\n\n  * •\n\nMechanistic breakthrough: Kinetic, spectroscopic and DFT studies provide definitive evidence for a parallel dual-substrate activation mechanism (“one-stone-two-birds”).\n\n  * •\n\nExceptional performance: Achieves a remarkable TON of 6095, and demonstrates outstanding practicality using simulated flue gas (23% CO_{2}) and high CO tolerance.\n\n  * •\n\nGeneralizable design principle: Bulky, rigid bidentate ligands create a flexible coordination cavity in multinuclear cores, offering a blueprint for dual-activation catalysts.\n\n## Abstract\n\nConventional catalytic processes are fundamentally limited by the “single-substrate activation” mechanism, which imposes a low theoretical ceiling on conversion efficiency per cycle. To overcome this bottleneck, we designed a Cu_{3} nanocluster catalyst equipped with a bulky, rigid bidentate ligand. This ligand engineering creates a flexible active site that enables parallel dual-substrate activation, defined as the simultaneous binding and equivalent activation of two substrate molecules, followed by their independent carboxylation with CO_{2}. This unique structure realizes a true “one-stone-two-birds” strategy (i.e., two parallel reactions proceed in one catalytic cycle, doubling the efficiency per active site). Consequently, this dual-activation mechanism surpasses the traditional efficiency limit, achieving a high turnover number (TON = 6095) under mild conditions (30 °C, 1  atm CO_{2}). Moreover, the system demonstrates strong industrial potential, evidenced by gram-scale synthesis, high CO tolerance, and > 98% conversion even using simulated flue gas containing only 23% CO_{2}.\n\n## Graphical abstract\n\nImage\n\n  1. Download: Download high-res image (79KB)\n  2. Download: Download full-size image\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n“One-Stone-Two-Birds” catalysis overcomes single-substrate activation: parallel dual-activation on a Cu3 nanocluter for efficient CO2 utilization - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0021951726003210)\nciteturn4search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Volume 461, September 2026, 116986 ... “One-Stone-Two-Birds” catalysis overcomes single-substrate activation: parallel dual-activation on a Cu_{3} nanocluter for efficient CO_{2} utilization\n\n## Journal of Catalysis\n\nVolume 461, September 2026, 116986\n\n# \n\nResearch article\n\n“One-Stone-Two-Birds” catalysis overcomes single-substrate activation: parallel dual-activation on a Cu_{3} nanocluter for efficient CO_{2} utilization\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.jcat.2026.116986Get rights and content\n\n## Highlights:\n\n  * •\n\nMechanistic breakthrough: Kinetics, spectroscopy and DFT prove parallel dual-substrate activation (“one-stone-two-birds”).\n\n  * •\n\nNotable performance: TON 6095, works in 23% CO_{2} flue gas, high CO tolerance.\n\n  * •\n\nDesign strategy: Bulky rigid bidentate ligands form flexible cavities for dual-activation catalysts.\n\n## Abstract\n\nConventional catalytic processes are fundamentally limited by the “single-substrate activation” mechanism, which imposes a low theoretical ceiling on conversion efficiency per cycle. To overcome this bottleneck, we designed a Cu_{3} nanocluster catalyst equipped with a bulky, rigid bidentate ligand. This ligand engineering creates a flexible active site that enables parallel dual-substrate activation, defined as the simultaneous binding and equivalent activation of two substrate molecules, followed by their independent carboxylation with CO_{2}. This unique structure realizes a true “one-stone-two-birds” strategy (i.e., two parallel reactions proceed in one catalytic cycle, doubling the efficiency per active site). Consequently, this dual-activation mechanism surpasses the traditional efficiency limit, achieving a high turnover number (TON = 6095) under mild conditions (30 °C, 1  atm CO_{2}). Moreover, the system demonstrates strong industrial potential, evidenced by gram-scale synthesis, high CO tolerance, and > 98% conversion even using simulated flue gas containing only 23% CO_{2}.\n\n## Graphical abstract\n\nImage\n\n  1. Download: Download high-res image (79KB)\n  2. Download: Download full-size image\n\n## Introduction\n\nFor decades, the pursuit of catalytic efficiency has been constrained by the paradigm of “single-substrate activation”, a mechanism that inherently caps the theoretical maximum turnover of each catalytic cycle (Scheme 1) [1], [2], [3], [4], [5], [6], [7]. The “one-stone-two-birds” strategy, which envisions the simultaneous activation of two substrate molecules at a single active site, offers a radical pathway to shatter this fundamental efficiency ceiling. Achieving this, however, imposes extreme structural demands on the catalyst's active center: it requires a precisely tailored geometry to accommodate two substrates without steric repulsion, coupled with a synchronized electronic environment to facilitate their stable and equivalent co-activation. Conventional catalysts with isolated active sites, such as single-atom catalysts or nanoparticles, typically lack the spatial flexibility and electronic cooperativity needed to meet such a requirement [8], [9], [10], [11], [12], [13], [14], [15].\n\nLow-nuclear metal nanoclusters represent as an ideal platform for this purpose. \nHerein, we address this challenge through rational ligand engineering. A low-nuclear Cu_{3} nanocluster featuring a bulky, rigid bidentate ligand has been successfully constructed. The dissociation of this ligand generates a flexible coordination pocket within the trinuclear copper core, transforming it into an integrated active site that can accommodate and equivalently activate two propargylic amines in parallel. For the first time, we demonstrate a clear “one-stone-two-birds” catalytic mechanism, in which a single catalytic event drives two parallel cyclization reactions with CO_{2}. This unique dual-activation pathway is unequivocally verified by kinetic studies, in situ mass spectrometry, and theoretical calculations. Under mild conditions, the catalyst achieves an outstanding turnover number (TON) of 6095, surpassing the theoretical limit of conventional single-substrate activation. Moreover, it exhibits exceptional CO tolerance, maintains high activity with dilute CO_{2} streams (e.g., >98% conversion using simulated flue gas containing only 23% CO_{2}), and proves scalable to gram-level synthesis. These attributes collectively address key challenges for industrial application. Beyond presenting a highly efficient catalyst, this work establishes a general ligand-engineering strategy for designing active sites capable of parallel dual-substrates activation, thereby opening new avenues to transcend the fundamental and practical limits of conventional catalysis\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAbout Us – Two Birds One Stone (https://twobirds-onestone.com/pages/about-us)\nciteturn4search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; After many requests from friends and family, Two Birds One Stone skincare was created, to help people like myself who want skincare products that 